    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3285da35-f6e9-58cc-9600-3fad9e873929",
      ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{4x-5}$ at the point where $x=1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=4x-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}={4}^{u}\\ln(4),\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} \\\\ & ={4}^{u}\\ln(4)\\cdot (4) \\\\ & =(4)\\cdot {4}^{u}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =4\\cdot {4}^{4x-5}\\ln(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\big|_{x=1} & =(4)\\cdot {4}^{4(1)-5}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =4\\cdot {4}^{-1}\\ln(4) \\\\ & =\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is $\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}.$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x=1$ is\n\n$$\ny_1={4}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1&=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{1}{4})&=(\\ln{\\left(4 \\right)})\\left(x-(1)\\right) \\\\ y&=2 x \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - 2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
